  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Tucaresol may interact with U373 MG through multiple pathways, with the putative gonadotropin-releasing hormone II receptor being a common target across different compounds. Tucaresol is reported to interact with this receptor, which is then targeted by (2S,3R,4S,5S)-2-[(2S,3R,4S,5R)-2-[[(1S,3R,6S,8R,9S,11S,12S,14S,15R,16R)-9,14-dihydroxy-15-[(2R,5S)-5-hydroxy-2,6,6-trimethyloxan-2-yl]-7,7,12,16-tetramethyl-6-pentacyclo[9.7.0.01,3.03,8.012,16]octadecanyl]oxy]-4,5-dihydroxyoxan-3-yl]oxyoxane-3,4,5-triol, which in turn targets U373 MG. Additionally, Tucaresol is reported to interact with 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which is also targeted by a complex compound and ultimately leads to U373 MG. These findings suggest that Tucaresol may exert its effects on U373 MG through multiple molecular mechanisms, possibly involving receptor signaling and proteasome regulation. The evidence is consistent with a role for Tucaresol in modulating cellular processes that affect U373 MG, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated."
